rmour ad that captures her efforts to break through the barriers of classical ballet has been viewed more than 8 million times on YouTube. She also has become a sensation on social media. She has more than 500,000 followers on Instagram and Twitter with pictures of her performing or relaxing in Under Armour tops and tights. Despite her star power on stage, Copeland told the sports network ESPN last year that the Under Armour deal pays her more than professional ballet. Her company's soloists typically made between $50,000 and $100,000 a year. Copeland has helped Under Armour, which got its start with men's football gear, capture a growing, yet underserved market. Women's sports apparel made up $600 million of Under Armour's $3 billion in revenue last year. "We believe our women's business can be as large if not bigger than our men's business, and we'll stick to that," said Kevin Plank, chief executive officer of Under Armour. Women represent more than 60 percent of the 130 million users of Under Armour's Connected Fitness app. Women have been crucial to the online fitness business that the retailer is spending wildly to win. ## "Tears Of Joy" For A Role Model After American Ballet Theatre announced Copeland's promotion to principal dancer, social media went wild. Congratulations poured in for her from companies, the ballet community and fans. Among them was television personality Star Jones. She wrote "crying tears of joy for @mistyonpointe and all the little girls she will inspire," referring to Copeland by her Twitter handle. And yet, as Copeland said, her accomplishments are still as important personally as they are publicly. "Every time I step onto the stage, it's not only proving to the audience that I'm capable, but to myself." </s>
