e was told she had failed two tests. One polygrapher had said she was lying about drug use. The second polygrapher said she passed. The first examiner may have made a mistake. Supervisors review the polygrapher's work. They say supervisors who reviewed the tests were not biased. They say government agencies are just making decisions based on the results of the tests. Experts say they do not know much more than the FBI. James Wedick was an FBI agent for 34 years. Now, he works as a private investigator in Sacramento, California. He said the FBI is very strict. Wedick said he has seen many highly qualified people turned away for failing the polygraph tests. The applicants had the chance to work as FBI agents. They had good resumes, but they were not allowed to work. The rejection stays with the applicants forever. They may have lost jobs elsewhere, also. They claimed they were not lying, which is unfair. In fact, half a dozen complainants thought they were discriminated against because they are African-American. ## "It's A Big Deal" David Mastagni, a lawyer, said the technical glitch is a real problem that could give people who have been denied jobs because of polygraph tests the chance to sue. Mastagni said manufacturers should be aware of the flaw. "If someone inaccurately got labeled a liar, that's a big deal," he said. "It could be lots of people lost out on a job." 
