# Growing Brand For Women In 2014, when Copeland made her debut as a principal with ABT, the "I Will What I Want" ad was created to promote the Under Armour brand to that growing female customer base. The company called the ad campaign its best financial performance "ever." The retailer now calls the ad campaign its most successful in history. Under Armour has grown very quickly to become the second-largest sportswear business in the United States. A spokesperson says that the growing women's market of women with higher spending power is important for Under Armour's future. More than 135 million people use Under Armour's fitness programs, which are available to watch online. Over 60 percent of those users are women, making them especially important for the company's growth. The success stories like Copeland's help Under Armour inspire new women to purchase the company's sports equipment and clothing. On social media, fans congratulated Copeland on her promotion and her growing celebrity. Among them was television personality Star Jones, who wrote, "crying tears of joy for [Copeland] and all the little girls who will be inspired." And yet, as Copeland told The Washington Post, her own accomplishments are still as important to her as they are to the public. "Every time I step onto the stage, it's not only proving to the audience that I'm capable but to myself," she explained. 
