iew books and documents in the vast reading room. The room is familiar to moviegoers who have seen "National Treasure: Book of Secrets" and "All the President's Men." One sign that pop culture sees the library as something other than a dusty, book-lined building is a new TV series, "The Librarians." The show revolves around a group at a library-like facility who have superpowers — protecting valuable artifacts from evil-doers. Billington doesn't have super powers, but his domain is spread over three buildings that include the National Archives and a satellite facility in rural Virginia. He has been the director of the library since 1987 and has introduced the National Book Festival, which has featured former First Lady Laura Bush. Billington also gives out awards celebrating American creativity. He oversees the cultural center where the Magna Carta — a historic 1215 British legal document — is on loan. The exhibition, will run through late January and also will display books from the library's own collection. The works of George Washington, the first president of the United States, are included. ## Famous Books And Artifacts Billington is a humble man. Although he was in the presence of the Queen — Princess Anne — and a collection of celebrities, he says that is not "all my time with royalty." "After we initially got involved...Billington became the principal reason that our interest in the Library of Congress intensified," Jerry Jones said in an email. Jones owns the Dallas Cowboys and Jerry Jones and his wife gave $1 million to the restoration of the Jefferson Library. This was to commemorate the library's 200th anniversary and coincided with the library's recent exhibition of a copy of the Magna Carta that he and his wife gave. "His dynamic and passionate leadership inspired us to complete the restoration of the Jefferson Library," Jones added. Billington said he is excited about a collaboration with photographer Carol Highsmith to document life in every state in