at's common on reservations. Twice a month, American Indians and Alaska Natives can come to the Chief Seattle Club and get help from lawyers. About half the cases are related to Native issues. Common ones are inheritance problems or trouble crossing over from the reservation. When it's a criminal case, the clinic can send clients to a criminal attorney. The lawyers come from different backgrounds but have one thing in common: They have knowledge of Indian law and the Indian experience. "For them to come to a safe place, with someone who has worked with Natives, is important," Lefferson said. "Otherwise, they might think someone won't understand them." ## An Issue Of Trust The idea began at the end of 2006, according to Lael Echo-Hawk, who was president of the Northwest Indian Bar Association. The group wanted to help a population that keeps to itself and doesn't trust other mainstream opportunities. The Chief Seattle Club was a logical place, said Echo-Hawk, a Pawnee. "As urban Indians, we know where to go and we know who the staff are," said Echo-Hawk, a Seattle lawyer. "Let's face it, if you have to meet with a lawyer, you are in a situation you don't want to be in, and it's uncomfortable. We wanted to help people solve problems." Ralph Forquera said he sees Native clients with long legal records at the Seattle Indian Health Board. That often comes from poverty. Their crimes didn't happen because they chose a life of crime, said Forquera, who comes from the Juaneno Band of California Mission Indians. It could be petty theft or public drunkeness instead. A city area also lacks the support base common on a reservation. Growing up on the Fond du Lac Reservation of Northern Minnesota, Dunlap never knew his family was poor. Lefferson's family didn't have running water on the Blackfoot Reservation, but everyone was poor. "In reservation poverty, you always had an auntie to feed you," Dunlap said. "Urban poverty for Natives is different." ## Helping With Social Services 